// What a field's permission governs: whether the field may be written.
//
// Flow stage. ErrorNoMut on an assignment is a data-flow diagnostic rather than
// a type-check one, despite reading like one -- core-flow-gate is where that is
// pinned.
//
// The permission was write-only state until recently. The one place that read
// it compared node pointers against the raw 'imm' singleton, and a permission
// written in source is a name use wrapping that singleton, so it never matched
// and an 'imm' field was writable. It asks the permission whether it may write
// now.
//
// A field's permission may be 'mut' or 'imm' and nothing else -- struct-parse
// holds the four that are refused. The two written out here are contrasted with
// one that writes none, which defaults to 'mut': per coneref/refstruct.html
// that means the container's permission governs, which is what makes the first
// two assignments legal.
//
// The second half of the file is the composition rule the manual calls
// viewpoint adaptation: what governs is the container's permission and the
// field's together, and either one saying no is enough. For the one thing a
// permission decides today -- whether a write is allowed -- that is already
// what the compiler does, and these are the cases that pin it. The rest of
// adaptation is unimplemented because the flags it would combine are read
// nowhere; workitems/Permissions carries what that leaves open.

struct Reading {
  inferred i32
  mut written i32
  imm frozen i32
}

fn writes() {
  mut r = Reading[1, 2, 3]

  // A field with no permission written defaults to 'mut', so the container's
  // permission is what governs, and this container is mutable
  r.inferred = 9

  // The same, said out loud
  r.written = 8

  // 'imm' is the field saying no, whatever the container permits
  r.frozen = 7              //~ ErrorNoMut:4 "You do not have permission to modify lval"
}

// The container saying no, over a field that says yes
fn throughValue() {
  imm frozen = Reading[1, 2, 3]
  frozen.written = 9        //~ ErrorNoMut "You do not have permission to modify lval"
}

fn throughRoRef(r &Reading) {
  r.written = 9             //~ ErrorNoMut "You do not have permission to modify lval"
}

// ... and the pair that shows it is both and not either: through a mutable
// reference the 'mut' field is writable and the 'imm' field is still not
fn throughMutRef(r &mut Reading) {
  r.written = 9
  r.frozen = 8              //~ ErrorNoMut "You do not have permission to modify lval"
}
